 The first thing we do is split all variables into a sum and a product of two terms.  If we factor $a_ia_j$ as $(a_i+a_j)a_j-a_j^2$, everything on the right is a sum.  This makes sense, since we can factor out the sum of two terms inside of the expression:

\begin{align*}
\sum_{1 \le i<j\le 100} a_ia_j &= \sum_{1 \le i<j\le 100} (a_i+a_j)a_j-a_j^2 \\
&= \sum_{i=1}^{100} \left(2i-1\right)a_i a_{101-i}\\ 
&\qquad - \sum_{j=1}^{100} a_j^2
\end{align*}Since $a_j^2 = 1$ for all $1 \le j \le 100$, this neatly simplifies to

\begin{align*}
\sum_{1 \le i<j\le 100} a_ia_j &= \sum_{i=1}^{100} (2i-1)a_i a_{101-i} - 100
\end{align*}We can hold $i$ constant in the inner sum, factor out that constant, and rewrite the inner sum a bit as follows:

\begin{align*}
\sum_{1 \le i<j\le 100} a_ia_j &= \sum_{i=1}^{100} (2i-1)a_i a_{101-i} - 100\\
&= (a_{100} a_1 + a_{99} a_2 + \dots + a_{51} a_{50}) + (3a_{100}a_2 + \dots + 99a_{50}a_{51}) + \dots - 100\\\
&=(100 a_{100}a_1) + (3a_{100}a_2 + 97a_{50}a_{51}) + \dots a_{50}a_{51}) - 100 \\
&= \sum_{j=1}^5 (101-2j) a_{101-j} a_j.\end{align*}This sum must be even (obviously), so it must differ by a positive multiple of 4 from a sum of odd numbers.  By picking $a_{1}, a_{4}, a_{7}, \dots,$ and $a_{97} = a_{100}$ to be 1, and all other terms to be $-1$, the inner sum on the right equals $<(101-2\cdot1), \dots, 101-2\cdot 50), (-1, -1, -1, -1)>$.  The inner sum simplifies thusly:

\begin{align*}
\sum_{j=1}^5 (101-2j) a_{101-j} a_j &= (101-2 \cdot 1) (1) + (101-2 \cdot 2 \cdot-1) + ...\\
&= 98 (1) -96(-1) - 94 (1) + ... -4 (1) + 2(-1)) \\
&= 6 \cdot 96 = 576\\
&= 4 \cdot 144
\end{align*}Thus, the sum $a_1 a_2 + ... + a_99a_{100} = \boxed{288}$ is the minimum given the conditions.

